From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent: an already-known STA is not a failed add
Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

wifi_add_sta() returns a struct sta pointer, but the "already in list"
branch ended with return 0 - a leftover from int semantics. The polled
refresh paths ignore the return value, so the only caller that noticed
was the wifi.sta connect-event handler, and it treats NULL as failure
and returns before stamld_update() and wifi_topology_notification().

A returning client whose previous entry survived in the model was
therefore never reported again. Measured on bpi-4g 2026-08-05 03:17:46:
an MLO client re-joined, all three legs answered "STA already in list",
the handler logged "Failed to add STA" three times, and a 23-minute
association earlier the same night (02:37-03:00) never produced a
single row in the controller database, while a legacy client on the
same node registered in under a second.

Return the entry we found. The event handler then refreshes the MLD
bookkeeping and sends the join notification exactly as it does for a
first-time client; duplicate joins are harmless because mld_add_sta()
already deduplicates and the controller write is an upsert.

--- a/src/agent.c
+++ b/src/agent.c
@@ -1659,7 +1659,13 @@
 				timer_del(&sptr->sta_steer_deauth_timer);
 
 			agent_sta_clean_rssi(sptr);
-			return 0;
+			/* This function returns a pointer, and 0 here made a healthy
+			 * "STA already known" indistinguishable from failure. The
+			 * connect-event handler bails on NULL, so a returning client
+			 * whose stale entry survived in the model was never reported
+			 * again: no stamld_update(), no Topology Notification.
+			 * Return the entry we found. */
+			return sptr;
 		} else {
 			struct netif_ap *oldap;
 
